[{:db/id "q-94908/selection/9e1a16035abfc00bc4ffce70994d756a4bd6938e7dd050f10bd7bee628e660f1" :answer/id #uuid "2864d932-5de9-50cd-917e-8b1c136da86f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94908/selection/814fa2487179f1752859879201b8a095b747d3b16513eb4380acd265db4e701d" :answer/id #uuid "1c0199c1-9fbc-58be-b632-9c2a35974053" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94908/selection/71b6129996b6d62f8a782bae2eb23e9d3d8a2fd674e51634a7275ef0885f491a" :answer/id #uuid "bee9c0a5-6d6b-5ffc-a22a-b6a09d9f409b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94908/selection/05fc2ce8ebe01e6ead0a3166d88a81c7d118527d17c8a7fe4e9f17a1d5f76f3f" :answer/id #uuid "76027cfb-47bb-5ada-915d-93c9459dd680" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94908/selection/792ff4ed1342d43c53c2d28e441dd70fb8204eb1ce7af2791d499b759fdec4c0" :answer/id #uuid "5e6304e9-50e8-53c6-90cf-6358439923bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94908/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "57dc18fa-db4b-51f7-bd70-5777ce626d72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94908/selection/71b6129996b6d62f8a782bae2eb23e9d3d8a2fd674e51634a7275ef0885f491a" :answer-field/choices ["q-94908/selection/9e1a16035abfc00bc4ffce70994d756a4bd6938e7dd050f10bd7bee628e660f1" "q-94908/selection/814fa2487179f1752859879201b8a095b747d3b16513eb4380acd265db4e701d" "q-94908/selection/71b6129996b6d62f8a782bae2eb23e9d3d8a2fd674e51634a7275ef0885f491a" "q-94908/selection/05fc2ce8ebe01e6ead0a3166d88a81c7d118527d17c8a7fe4e9f17a1d5f76f3f" "q-94908/selection/792ff4ed1342d43c53c2d28e441dd70fb8204eb1ce7af2791d499b759fdec4c0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94908" :question/id #uuid "07181131-caae-520e-9ffd-8a571dcaad72" :question/math-academy-id "q-94908" :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94908/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a9a1aa0-8822-5dbd-8534-254ace55c555"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94908"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140987/selection/c83a6a0f73396595d2af593c595dacd25ec081bcb2c44a2ac06f835e421b1a72" :answer/id #uuid "90dea696-81e9-5ef7-9fbf-4f7bef5df96f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140987/selection/88245df3924e22e42e0099ccf3a6ea0cd58f85096cd5b8330308d23c015d99dc" :answer/id #uuid "6fc0633a-7638-5a41-bdb3-d7441141c435" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140987/selection/45cf111dd32c843840bd12a03b5f10e6204c95e7c575e6bc11023e4aced48642" :answer/id #uuid "bc4a2702-aa5d-5fb1-b0fe-3b79eb0d05ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140987/selection/a214cc7ec846319864f5e32664668e4434be821731defdfbea9f781da7b97655" :answer/id #uuid "4c0572aa-52b0-5bbb-a876-9df50af5e65a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140987/selection/c977624d84f70480b05f248375e340be7580707f5f06ee6509ece30e864058b8" :answer/id #uuid "45dada6c-7f87-5235-83bd-93d961646b03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140987/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "42b396d5-8731-5330-92fa-4be67c53c532" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140987/selection/a214cc7ec846319864f5e32664668e4434be821731defdfbea9f781da7b97655" :answer-field/choices ["q-140987/selection/c83a6a0f73396595d2af593c595dacd25ec081bcb2c44a2ac06f835e421b1a72" "q-140987/selection/88245df3924e22e42e0099ccf3a6ea0cd58f85096cd5b8330308d23c015d99dc" "q-140987/selection/45cf111dd32c843840bd12a03b5f10e6204c95e7c575e6bc11023e4aced48642" "q-140987/selection/a214cc7ec846319864f5e32664668e4434be821731defdfbea9f781da7b97655" "q-140987/selection/c977624d84f70480b05f248375e340be7580707f5f06ee6509ece30e864058b8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140987" :question/id #uuid "7b1133d5-5872-572e-8342-de6cddf5444d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140987" :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n4{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-140987/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a9a1aa0-8822-5dbd-8534-254ace55c555"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140987"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94898/selection/9b419aeeb00612a7132a402c16524633eb0722966dfc5629a228c7ffc4b8fe0c" :answer/id #uuid "68231d48-b7a9-50ef-846e-3b8dd02c4584" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94898/selection/faec501954fe2aa9ea0f7de487c81d476d2b0f7b3fad33afb26f07faae053791" :answer/id #uuid "0ad3475c-e37c-589a-8d5f-cd8a5eddc2db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94898/selection/6b8a1cf1d17b88ba3fdf8aef4ead49a11d52e28316da7ca53c37efea43f6346f" :answer/id #uuid "98c72e1c-d475-5754-a14d-8e4d3a42992d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94898/selection/05fbe98a6e72098d9a0c4bceb30212f585b844dc935a9c29068fe103c43555af" :answer/id #uuid "4c997789-8859-5c29-b117-e43de29abd90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94898/selection/ee867aeeb37ad3e8ace375da6c6a13edc4f1f9ff8eca7d3632630e29647d8a86" :answer/id #uuid "ee53b4bd-9aa6-5905-9afa-6591f3285b02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94898/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d37f18d4-2a54-5979-a83d-6bdce01512fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94898/selection/ee867aeeb37ad3e8ace375da6c6a13edc4f1f9ff8eca7d3632630e29647d8a86" :answer-field/choices ["q-94898/selection/9b419aeeb00612a7132a402c16524633eb0722966dfc5629a228c7ffc4b8fe0c" "q-94898/selection/faec501954fe2aa9ea0f7de487c81d476d2b0f7b3fad33afb26f07faae053791" "q-94898/selection/6b8a1cf1d17b88ba3fdf8aef4ead49a11d52e28316da7ca53c37efea43f6346f" "q-94898/selection/05fbe98a6e72098d9a0c4bceb30212f585b844dc935a9c29068fe103c43555af" "q-94898/selection/ee867aeeb37ad3e8ace375da6c6a13edc4f1f9ff8eca7d3632630e29647d8a86"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94898" :question/id #uuid "e5eb3325-8950-5c51-91d6-e1c0a28e3d5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94898" :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-94898/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a9a1aa0-8822-5dbd-8534-254ace55c555"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94898"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-94446"] :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-94449"] :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-9) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-1,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n4{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{3}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-140997/selection/1ee02da260e7c5a58934a801efd9f860ca06c299d210b3d50bc11ba3afabbcae" :answer/id #uuid "cd1f2e33-eb57-5e09-b215-e048e45c6cfe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140997/selection/e4279352324f010d6d88a6c62a9142d4b2a2e3156cd25db6c4b660e805a41dc4" :answer/id #uuid "fdf59f1d-ae0b-55af-a225-2271d6cec7c2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140997/selection/8e339d40cb94b90d4885536184eaa9364cd571ddec258f09b391fb7c7fea2fa1" :answer/id #uuid "d1641326-3a1d-5f82-b577-063020e607d4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140997/selection/84c0ff886be6043b09aab34fd9da74ab4993f294e77fc7dafb1ad625a0a8c445" :answer/id #uuid "dd20683e-c621-5ce2-8614-5bb5813ae2a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140997/selection/9d91a1af4a2a39a9b6ed0304866c593f0d21a012baf0d6e5ebc5b827f87133b4" :answer/id #uuid "1bd1d5fa-2f8e-5b7d-b369-fe53c27e995b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140997/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "45eb3de3-d29a-56c9-8763-211b7ec9c628" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140997/selection/8e339d40cb94b90d4885536184eaa9364cd571ddec258f09b391fb7c7fea2fa1" :answer-field/choices ["q-140997/selection/1ee02da260e7c5a58934a801efd9f860ca06c299d210b3d50bc11ba3afabbcae" "q-140997/selection/e4279352324f010d6d88a6c62a9142d4b2a2e3156cd25db6c4b660e805a41dc4" "q-140997/selection/8e339d40cb94b90d4885536184eaa9364cd571ddec258f09b391fb7c7fea2fa1" "q-140997/selection/84c0ff886be6043b09aab34fd9da74ab4993f294e77fc7dafb1ad625a0a8c445" "q-140997/selection/9d91a1af4a2a39a9b6ed0304866c593f0d21a012baf0d6e5ebc5b827f87133b4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140997" :question/id #uuid "2172cdc2-fb35-5393-84ba-3c3dbc3c7926" :question/math-academy-id "q-140997" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =6,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140997/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1bee282-2775-59f5-a929-9dc5327703bc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140997"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-95071/selection/585fece83ed4b140ee6fb65eff140724788c3a4d6b21bffef11570d9733f511f" :answer/id #uuid "145e589c-06c9-5aea-92dd-9b741dfb469b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95071/selection/140cba0b4f3ca71456d5852bd8bbf053495b50bd0b7627b1ff1a5a439a6a0a9e" :answer/id #uuid "7e0ee0ff-e81d-53a1-ba15-5356231bc4f1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95071/selection/b7a542eb8261da03b9ac09520c0faaa34b8036f7ee34fc079c22a642c389006f" :answer/id #uuid "153c2b85-c4c8-5f6a-b238-3345ba47e53e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95071/selection/7f12e72783ac100992652fe663b0abdba375399a6464d52bda48f5717d213c8b" :answer/id #uuid "72585252-4b88-568a-a0b4-78d669818c70" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95071/selection/6b07097a41300fc9985012cf12d31abf42f63452868e664d6b1a1c5a12a981d7" :answer/id #uuid "f8c8af81-1533-5a16-a3af-ae8125c77e15" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95071/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5c79817a-42bd-537a-b141-1650ecaa82f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-95071/selection/b7a542eb8261da03b9ac09520c0faaa34b8036f7ee34fc079c22a642c389006f" :answer-field/choices ["q-95071/selection/585fece83ed4b140ee6fb65eff140724788c3a4d6b21bffef11570d9733f511f" "q-95071/selection/140cba0b4f3ca71456d5852bd8bbf053495b50bd0b7627b1ff1a5a439a6a0a9e" "q-95071/selection/b7a542eb8261da03b9ac09520c0faaa34b8036f7ee34fc079c22a642c389006f" "q-95071/selection/7f12e72783ac100992652fe663b0abdba375399a6464d52bda48f5717d213c8b" "q-95071/selection/6b07097a41300fc9985012cf12d31abf42f63452868e664d6b1a1c5a12a981d7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-95071" :question/id #uuid "51bd93f1-00c1-504d-a9b3-5e4ed39fa2fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-95071" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -40 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-(-8)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-95071/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1bee282-2775-59f5-a929-9dc5327703bc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-95071"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-95069/selection/ef444b6ed8ff0922c798cbf7ee96f311eddb062945ff174ab5d489bf5ba89207" :answer/id #uuid "0b3aad92-db58-5c42-8e64-68a3bdb45c21" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95069/selection/4fd38c341e511eff6e54da81a2fe31b3314ddc1451bbe146b0ae6f603714d298" :answer/id #uuid "fb2efc1f-3c2b-5d96-a033-376c4d15b16a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95069/selection/b65bd069205e1edf01d70bcaad8e9f5f9f1f44c6a37aa4450bcf81d887bf7b55" :answer/id #uuid "7c69188d-583c-5316-ab22-f684a5a9f558" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95069/selection/5b256da48ef0b0308200ba2dd86b038812c54f7c223dbd3dfadc19667c985a9a" :answer/id #uuid "9ac40c2e-e06c-56ee-957d-02e9c30c340c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95069/selection/50860e35ee580834fbf5116b0ea9371babcb860336ddd2c43c6098bd01df9f84" :answer/id #uuid "2dad5629-755a-5608-86ca-69bba62e6479" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95069/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5f33e86c-8e79-54ce-a7ef-f76561e538f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-95069/selection/50860e35ee580834fbf5116b0ea9371babcb860336ddd2c43c6098bd01df9f84" :answer-field/choices ["q-95069/selection/ef444b6ed8ff0922c798cbf7ee96f311eddb062945ff174ab5d489bf5ba89207" "q-95069/selection/4fd38c341e511eff6e54da81a2fe31b3314ddc1451bbe146b0ae6f603714d298" "q-95069/selection/b65bd069205e1edf01d70bcaad8e9f5f9f1f44c6a37aa4450bcf81d887bf7b55" "q-95069/selection/5b256da48ef0b0308200ba2dd86b038812c54f7c223dbd3dfadc19667c985a9a" "q-95069/selection/50860e35ee580834fbf5116b0ea9371babcb860336ddd2c43c6098bd01df9f84"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-95069" :question/id #uuid "0d232faf-2de0-5a39-91fc-eea4fa2a2bb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-95069" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=-3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-3-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2,\\,-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-4)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-95069/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1bee282-2775-59f5-a929-9dc5327703bc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-95069"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-95092/selection/2e0dffd8aa5a3e58cefa3cb5b5d3735975431c0ef5fa133fb1d0202b2ef58f46" :answer/id #uuid "f51d6454-5243-5ffe-9a8d-269bfe8bbb6f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95092/selection/7eb8b576f6f647795c66c027917b2c68b561cae114d5a17d2bf3d3ad0fd19360" :answer/id #uuid "02d60d25-29bc-5e0f-9a69-4a7b17099c96" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -15\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95092/selection/d4aa2edec53ef69d1e0e7521845d1aeaae153470afb134e6d7f85daddbd477c6" :answer/id #uuid "a289ee0c-7af9-5c41-b053-4a0e18cfb470" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95092/selection/7638b7af4602ef608cf30ee6d0ebd6686047f4d578d14cf0715b9f1d242a5be3" :answer/id #uuid "826ebd89-e64c-5d6b-8d0b-3eec48c867bb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -15\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95092/selection/56d623bb10c818d0beb68571715071bfd1904e1935149bc9f897c950e8cc5fc1" :answer/id #uuid "514dd2eb-445e-5d2d-98d1-043c886394e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95092/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "41be5ff6-7520-52cd-a14e-5b648f456578" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-95092/selection/56d623bb10c818d0beb68571715071bfd1904e1935149bc9f897c950e8cc5fc1" :answer-field/choices ["q-95092/selection/2e0dffd8aa5a3e58cefa3cb5b5d3735975431c0ef5fa133fb1d0202b2ef58f46" "q-95092/selection/7eb8b576f6f647795c66c027917b2c68b561cae114d5a17d2bf3d3ad0fd19360" "q-95092/selection/d4aa2edec53ef69d1e0e7521845d1aeaae153470afb134e6d7f85daddbd477c6" "q-95092/selection/7638b7af4602ef608cf30ee6d0ebd6686047f4d578d14cf0715b9f1d242a5be3" "q-95092/selection/56d623bb10c818d0beb68571715071bfd1904e1935149bc9f897c950e8cc5fc1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-95092" :question/id #uuid "84da4bfe-23c6-56a5-9883-ca3f2fee6613" :question/math-academy-id "q-95092" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}14-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-20\\lambda +75 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =15,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=15,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-15I & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]-15[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-15I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-95092/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1bee282-2775-59f5-a929-9dc5327703bc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-95092"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140998/selection/bca9b29d01cc5487ca39a33dea4e7dff9ce4d67b676bda9b4ef2b583d51673f8" :answer/id #uuid "ea1181ab-306d-55b2-892d-0b7918f50c13" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140998/selection/ed103a6a5655a2aeb3d7665cfe082d6c10c34e9278fdf909fa0fbc1a860b9d21" :answer/id #uuid "1963adc5-9bac-55a5-b8e0-9eea9c095442" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140998/selection/fe074277b318f200e6594c4abac6cc65dba3f189019c99194ae6111242870fee" :answer/id #uuid "20d0544f-c482-5dd5-98f9-b641127e854b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140998/selection/f289c5b3845589c34211741610696a8e843f2cc3d073da18286506ead74bfd12" :answer/id #uuid "d843cfb8-3036-582e-9353-20ddeee40ca4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140998/selection/2abaf92b3b6ef4f134d35e29ad428ab0eb7d67eafa183a7318bbb21364f0c188" :answer/id #uuid "380a56bf-13ca-526a-a152-21969eeed029" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140998/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b3fbda42-9fc6-5c6b-8e87-1d3901292df0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140998/selection/bca9b29d01cc5487ca39a33dea4e7dff9ce4d67b676bda9b4ef2b583d51673f8" :answer-field/choices ["q-140998/selection/bca9b29d01cc5487ca39a33dea4e7dff9ce4d67b676bda9b4ef2b583d51673f8" "q-140998/selection/ed103a6a5655a2aeb3d7665cfe082d6c10c34e9278fdf909fa0fbc1a860b9d21" "q-140998/selection/fe074277b318f200e6594c4abac6cc65dba3f189019c99194ae6111242870fee" "q-140998/selection/f289c5b3845589c34211741610696a8e843f2cc3d073da18286506ead74bfd12" "q-140998/selection/2abaf92b3b6ef4f134d35e29ad428ab0eb7d67eafa183a7318bbb21364f0c188"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140998" :question/id #uuid "f92c48bb-fec5-5fb3-9d76-72c2d4d25f64" :question/math-academy-id "q-140998" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =10,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\sqrt{1}0}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140998/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1bee282-2775-59f5-a929-9dc5327703bc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140998"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-95103/selection/bd9091eb850974ff521a1387479708651fa43bd73644a0fccf950a242940ebce" :answer/id #uuid "461cb8cf-68ed-5217-b4e2-6fe7fb1166d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95103/selection/59dc723bf89a511217ab10147d6592894b93dfb9429215e287190b88dd1013a4" :answer/id #uuid "e4f79a49-d35b-5f28-98ba-45540a6debe6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95103/selection/fe6866bfc82f54c9f432b01305ceace707aca6c87472b321730a9aa6738d40a1" :answer/id #uuid "45f2ac83-3133-5a86-8ec9-4a30719ff6fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95103/selection/6dcc5d14d1ec31847fd855a21de8b1b44979269e580fad88a6ce00b829c82af6" :answer/id #uuid "ce621e29-9ed2-5486-92db-82f88cfa4755" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95103/selection/0fc0ff2e4c5f39ed6626d899cb2dc5033cbe7ad932055a6bb358a7f7d56862d6" :answer/id #uuid "3a452dd8-1550-5791-af0e-2127a6736717" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95103/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a1f4916-89aa-5a99-b8ae-6dc757f93c9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-95103/selection/6dcc5d14d1ec31847fd855a21de8b1b44979269e580fad88a6ce00b829c82af6" :answer-field/choices ["q-95103/selection/bd9091eb850974ff521a1387479708651fa43bd73644a0fccf950a242940ebce" "q-95103/selection/59dc723bf89a511217ab10147d6592894b93dfb9429215e287190b88dd1013a4" "q-95103/selection/fe6866bfc82f54c9f432b01305ceace707aca6c87472b321730a9aa6738d40a1" "q-95103/selection/6dcc5d14d1ec31847fd855a21de8b1b44979269e580fad88a6ce00b829c82af6" "q-95103/selection/0fc0ff2e4c5f39ed6626d899cb2dc5033cbe7ad932055a6bb358a7f7d56862d6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-95103" :question/id #uuid "fb0b43c2-0c8a-5ba6-b111-27a3f0d7a440" :question/math-academy-id "q-95103" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=0.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-95103/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07cbbfc-0f61-55e8-afe9-b675dc74f24e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-95103"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-95100/selection/1e4fe79bd8e07d5a45724f538862b76100146e1989f1775976a790870f4db79f" :answer/id #uuid "d2ec7036-d2a0-5ff9-ae60-c885663ae14b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95100/selection/bfffcbfc5d089daf0995462c85ea5e7a0da29fa330df20333f05d4ce652c88ee" :answer/id #uuid "9dac2610-f8fd-55b0-80c9-5b9dcdc3e80b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95100/selection/7ac62f0669d22375ced1f5355dcfe152cd1174235a2c5f0d33a536f6fc93f9da" :answer/id #uuid "1c78ed55-6ae2-52ba-ab00-48d5ca9c33ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95100/selection/c2d540f9cd27d3f9ddb6956a0a94fda78ef43b84a89c91d2c537bdc681043d5f" :answer/id #uuid "70e57bfc-9186-5950-8e9b-8ac2be8429fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95100/selection/1819253e1f66008eb4850bf2060b0763bdec8ce60a18f8fb67a6633ba5e08977" :answer/id #uuid "b1fde2c3-9b6c-5ea9-aa9e-8df95de6194a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95100/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b8d36d72-b7cb-5e79-a3d3-73d9b22c696d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-95100/selection/c2d540f9cd27d3f9ddb6956a0a94fda78ef43b84a89c91d2c537bdc681043d5f" :answer-field/choices ["q-95100/selection/1e4fe79bd8e07d5a45724f538862b76100146e1989f1775976a790870f4db79f" "q-95100/selection/bfffcbfc5d089daf0995462c85ea5e7a0da29fa330df20333f05d4ce652c88ee" "q-95100/selection/7ac62f0669d22375ced1f5355dcfe152cd1174235a2c5f0d33a536f6fc93f9da" "q-95100/selection/c2d540f9cd27d3f9ddb6956a0a94fda78ef43b84a89c91d2c537bdc681043d5f" "q-95100/selection/1819253e1f66008eb4850bf2060b0763bdec8ce60a18f8fb67a6633ba5e08977"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-95100" :question/id #uuid "f3b5dfdd-d519-5388-b5ca-0b0271c9801c" :question/math-academy-id "q-95100" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-95100/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07cbbfc-0f61-55e8-afe9-b675dc74f24e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-95100"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-95102/selection/c43a39d5aadfe8c783ca89b621214d4ae002bbe08f850bb98147e254fbf54ed0" :answer/id #uuid "75494d25-e44b-5fd0-a2a0-4fe0322b0959" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95102/selection/7ac62f0669d22375ced1f5355dcfe152cd1174235a2c5f0d33a536f6fc93f9da" :answer/id #uuid "28990e21-35a7-5d8c-98fb-1909f0c6f409" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95102/selection/fe6866bfc82f54c9f432b01305ceace707aca6c87472b321730a9aa6738d40a1" :answer/id #uuid "771b7a96-6238-5272-ae1f-eb1cda4d0683" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95102/selection/cbb32393b4d534c3eddd88573d78178c9962de0061c5990a716e6bc4da66ce4f" :answer/id #uuid "63f5980e-4efb-5d6a-a8a6-669efa4942c8" :an