formations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-95102/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07cbbfc-0f61-55e8-afe9-b675dc74f24e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-95102"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-94461"] :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=3.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-95101/selection/fe6866bfc82f54c9f432b01305ceace707aca6c87472b321730a9aa6738d40a1" :answer/id #uuid "01caabcc-bdab-5558-8e12-fe162041ffa7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95101/selection/7ac62f0669d22375ced1f5355dcfe152cd1174235a2c5f0d33a536f6fc93f9da" :answer/id #uuid "e8bfcef9-7f41-5da3-8bbe-1f4b023b2632" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95101/selection/2914b5c3efbad72fc2323990f7fd890990462bc3ee792357a80d5c22da7f100a" :answer/id #uuid "75fb51e7-f673-59b1-bf06-c48b7d024a34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95101/selection/5c2d049645cf84db4b6f7fdb45831e5a4278ca2237e83ead3483d3b6db1b15ed" :answer/id #uuid "19f09a3a-677b-5a4b-811b-ad1df28ea253" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95101/selection/fbe89a56e3c070ffd1481ac17244532cece75508941a7cf0fcb32890448a2b07" :answer/id #uuid "d5746d10-e2f9-53ec-8b2c-bc1c73ffbb2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95101/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "dade6dfb-7edd-58a7-aceb-d64bd8b0da8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-95101/selection/7ac62f0669d22375ced1f5355dcfe152cd1174235a2c5f0d33a536f6fc93f9da" :answer-field/choices ["q-95101/selection/fe6866bfc82f54c9f432b01305ceace707aca6c87472b321730a9aa6738d40a1" "q-95101/selection/7ac62f0669d22375ced1f5355dcfe152cd1174235a2c5f0d33a536f6fc93f9da" "q-95101/selection/2914b5c3efbad72fc2323990f7fd890990462bc3ee792357a80d5c22da7f100a" "q-95101/selection/5c2d049645cf84db4b6f7fdb45831e5a4278ca2237e83ead3483d3b6db1b15ed" "q-95101/selection/fbe89a56e3c070ffd1481ac17244532cece75508941a7cf0fcb32890448a2b07"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-95101" :question/id #uuid "1cb2c63c-3cd4-5b0f-9b7a-5452eee71130" :question/math-academy-id "q-95101" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-95101/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07cbbfc-0f61-55e8-afe9-b675dc74f24e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-95101"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8249"] :question/problem "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+x_2^2+2x_3^2+4x_1x_2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/44753acffd8b6384cd291f9b0758edd7676acb319caa22d5b07be36dcf72a5a6.png)\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,2,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/217b5d7d3b82800bdfc44d83ca8b068e02a37759e52b7a8382505bfb102f753f.png)\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3y_1^2 + 2y_2^2 - y_3^2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8250"] :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2-2x_1x_2+2x_2^2,$ find its canonical (diagonal) form $\\mathbf{y}^T \\! D \\mathbf{y}$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/69dcf821f3ff202b567b901d8f512c5cecf7159617ca51a0664c14a5099e9715.png)\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/fc9465d4e5bcd43a3c29e5946b05ec18cdbc7453a4011aa548f374016c561b45.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/59c1bd46325fab8a52fed162d7ae47e8fb33a10cc524cd770a3b18f5934e51dd.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/adc9241219f3b5ebce0d8418bf34e250b7230ea612fabca5bdfc109c98a18dbb.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/dcab437eb77b29ab5bcd5c3ae14b77f10f01382aafadf2aecded1bb1fb72dc32.png)\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/3f6b1002893290dde6c9a68425b0035e249ff3630a41d29c9da5756c1a18f6a6.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8251"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/6135fe578745ebf2eccb4370557f0dba6ec27361a192e4e87536eadad87a39bf.png)\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ to the canonical (diagonal) form $\\mathbf{y}^T \\! D \\mathbf{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=4, \\quad$ $\\lambda_2=2, \\quad$ and $\\quad\\lambda_3=1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For $\\lambda_1=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/13eee082d48c56e6c7e796549eddc0185868b39841a7c5946ce9ce9e996ba553.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/1e7c81e373377bd719e1bbc9e87a8c88d13f6116bcf2949897fc26246e2265d1.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/61311029da90b54253a552ce2198ce9193d7f343c3ab345eea25419444da00a3.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/3bf5334469b8b135548dfbd0c35725ee6927e2a169c6db37a1b0bae3f62695e5.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_3=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/1cb1b34b0df191e9fa63a907ba59e26a2ad16f3977e97d5b8588dc5e51f2c3a0.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_3$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_3  \\| = 1,$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/39bf82ed6be834f41140a70e048268b39d47d7a0944461247d312d4efe95d184.png)\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/assets/fb5604c6dc892a34af523d6b9b769cd50c59e82ed65d7be47cb89ed6044125bf.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
